course: dictionary
lesson: 12312
title: Correlated: things that change together
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how two things are linked. «Correlated» is one of them. When two things are correlated, they tend to change together: when one goes up, the other usually goes up too, or when one goes up, the other goes down.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Hours of practice and test scores are correlated.» More practice usually goes with higher scores, so the two change together. Correlated must mean linked in a way that makes them change together.
explain: As a verb, correlated means compared two things to see how they match. «The scientist correlated the temperature with ice-cream sales» means she checked whether hot days and ice-cream sales went together. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «compared to see how they match» still make sense? Yes, so you have it.
fact: Correlated means linked, so that two things tend to change together.
fact: «Positively correlated» means both things rise together, or both fall together.
fact: «Negatively correlated» means one thing rises while the other falls.
fact: Correlation does not mean causation: two things changing together does not prove that one causes the other.
fact: On a graph, scientists look for correlation by checking whether the dots show a pattern.
exercise: «Rain and umbrella sales are correlated.» What does correlated mean here? | *They change together | They are both very expensive | They never happen at the same time | They are the same colour
exercise: «The scientist correlated the temperature with ice-cream sales.» What did the scientist do? | Made the temperature go up | *Compared two things to see how they match | Wrote a poem about the weather | Ate ice cream all day
exercise: On a graph, one number goes up while the other goes down. What is the best word for this? | positively correlated | *negatively correlated | luminous | ancient
test:
«Shoe size and height are strongly correlated.» What does strongly correlated suggest? | *The two are very closely linked | The two are not linked at all | The two are very large | The two are always equal
test:
Adam says, «Our two numbers are correlated, so one must cause the other.» Is he right? | *No, correlation does not prove cause | Yes, correlated always means cause | Yes, if the numbers are big | No, because correlated means equal
exam:
Which sentence best describes two things that are correlated? | *They change together, so one tends to rise or fall with the other | They are exactly the same size | They never happen at the same time | They are both made of metal
